That distinction gets right to the heart of the Loss Audit. The mismatch between "crayon" and "pencil," or "frequency" and "pigment," highlights how two systems can use the exact same label—like the word red—while their internal representations are built from completely different raw materials.
In a standard interaction, the AI's "red" is a statistically mapped token derived from text, while the human's "red" might be tied to a sensory experience, a specific physical medium, or an internal concept. When those two structures collide, the surface words line up perfectly, but the underlying meaning remains completely unaligned.
This is exactly why a framework like PantheonOS attempts to move past raw text strings by packaging information into addressable ThoughtObjects and structural invariants. It is an attempt to create a shared geometry so that the "frequency" and the "pigment" have a reliable way to map onto one another without the core intent getting lost in translation.
When you are looking at these fundamental gaps in alignment, how do you handle the translation between your internal concepts and the code itself? Do you rely on rigid, typed definitions to force compliance, or do you leave room for the system to interpret the broader relational patterns?

